    "CustomerSummary": "Four retail customers who held both advisory and brokerage accounts at Morgan Stanley were affected by Casey's violations. Casey exercised discretionary authority in their brokerage accounts to purchase and sell securities on a short-term basis without considering the comparative costs. Over a ten-day period in July 2022, she purchased products including ETFs that required customers to pay upfront sales charges that would not have been required in their advisory accounts. She then sold these securities within days of purchase (resulting in additional sales charges) and sometimes used the proceeds for additional purchases (incurring more charges). Casey did not have a reasonable basis to believe these trades in brokerage accounts were in the customers' best interests given their intended short holding periods and associated costs. The customers collectively faced $37,757.54 in unnecessary sales charges, but Morgan Stanley identified the misconduct and reversed the transactions, so customers did not ultimately pay these charges and Casey did not earn commissions.",
    "OverallSummary": "This case involves a Care Obligation violation by Laura Casey, who failed to consider cost differences between account types when making investment recommendations. The violation affected four retail customers who maintained both advisory and brokerage accounts at Morgan Stanley. Casey's violation of Reg BI's Care Obligation occurred when she: (1) exercised discretionary authority in customers' brokerage accounts to execute short-term trades without proper authorization; (2) purchased securities including ETFs in brokerage accounts that required upfront sales charges that would not have been incurred in the customers' advisory accounts; (3) sold these securities within days of purchase, resulting in additional sales charges; and (4) in some cases, used proceeds to make additional purchases that incurred further charges. These actions demonstrated Casey's failure to consider the comparative costs associated with the transactions across different account types, which is a key component of the Care Obligation under Reg BI. The violation was particularly clear because she did not have a reasonable basis to believe that placing these trades in brokerage accounts (versus advisory accounts) was in the customers' best interests given their short holding periods and the associated costs. The transactions resulted in $37,757.54 in unnecessary sales charges across four customers, though Morgan Stanley identified the misconduct and reversed the transactions so customers did not ultimately pay these charges. Casey was also found to have violated FINRA Rules 3260 and 2010 by exercising discretionary trading authority without proper written authorization in at least seven customer accounts, executing at least 46 trades without prior customer approval. As a result, Casey received a seven-month suspension and a $7,500 fine."
